        "knowledge_point": "Solving a System of Equations Using Back Substitution",
        "problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=1 \\\\  & {x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=-1 \\\\  & {x}_{3}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "worked_solution": "The third equation states that ${x}_{3}=3.$ Substitute this into the second equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-(3)=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-1+3=2.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=3$ and ${x}_{2}=2$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(2)+(3)=1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=1-3+2=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=0$,  ${x}_{2}=2$, and ${x}_{3}=3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨0,2,3⟩.$",
